{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-65497",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The constant $D$ is the vertical shift of the function.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14256025/assets/8188e00efabf5e26d7be8478e65cd434fda3f632148c3f9afab5afc5620a53da.png)\n\nTo plot the given graph, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\n- Vertically stretch it with scale factor $3$ to give $y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\n- Then, shift it by $1$ unit down to give $y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-1.$\n\nTherefore $D=-1.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
